::ENG1_U03_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Community cleanups can improve shared spaces and build neighborhood pride. Volunteers remove litter from parks, repaint benches, and plant flowers near sidewalks. After the cleanup, residents often report that the area feels safer and more welcoming. Question: What is the central idea? {
=A. Community cleanups can improve places and strengthen pride.#Correct. This answer captures the main idea developed by the details about cleaner spaces and neighborhood pride.
~B. Benches are usually painted in parks.#Incorrect. This is only one detail and does not express the passage's full central idea.
~C. Flowers grow near sidewalks in every town.#Incorrect. The passage gives flowers as one example, not a universal claim.
~D. Volunteers should never work together.#Incorrect. The passage shows volunteers working together successfully.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Community cleanups can improve shared spaces and build neighborhood pride. Volunteers remove litter from parks, repaint benches, and plant flowers near sidewalks. After the cleanup, residents often report that the area feels safer and more welcoming. Question: Which detail best supports the central idea? {
~A. Some parks have benches.#Incorrect. This is related to parks, but it is too general to support the full central idea.
=B. Residents often report that the area feels safer and more welcoming.#Correct. This detail directly supports the idea that cleanups improve spaces and build pride.
~C. Sidewalks are used by pedestrians.#Incorrect. This fact is not in the passage and does not support the central idea.
~D. Flowers can be many colors.#Incorrect. This may be true, but it does not support the main point about community cleanups.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Reading aloud can help younger students develop fluency. When students hear smooth reading, they learn how punctuation affects expression. Repeated read-aloud practice can also help students recognize common words more quickly. Question: Why is the punctuation detail strong evidence? {
~A. It changes the topic from reading to math.#Incorrect. The detail stays on reading because punctuation affects how text is read aloud.
~B. It is unrelated to fluency.#Incorrect. Expression and punctuation are part of fluent reading.
=C. It explains a specific way read-aloud practice supports fluency.#Correct. The detail shows how hearing smooth reading helps students understand expression.
~D. It proves students should never read silently.#Incorrect. The passage does not make that claim.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: A school breakfast program can support student readiness. Students who eat before class may have more energy, and teachers may see fewer late-morning complaints about hunger. Question: Which evidence would be weakest support? {
~A. Students who eat before class may have more energy.#Incorrect. This directly supports readiness because energy affects the school day.
~B. Teachers may see fewer hunger complaints.#Incorrect. This supports the central idea by showing a classroom effect.
~C. Breakfast can affect student readiness.#Incorrect. This restates the central idea rather than giving detailed evidence, but it is still related.
=D. The cafeteria walls are painted blue.#Correct. Wall color does not explain how breakfast supports readiness.
}

::ENG1_U03_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.06] Passage: Bike lanes can make city travel safer for cyclists. Painted lanes give riders a clear space, and signs remind drivers to watch for bicycles. In some areas, protected lanes use barriers to separate cyclists from cars. Question: Which statement best evaluates the evidence? {
~A. The evidence is weak because it gives no examples.#Incorrect. The passage gives examples such as painted lanes, signs, and protected barriers.
=B. The evidence is strong because it gives specific safety features.#Correct. The details explain concrete ways bike lanes can improve safety.
~C. The evidence is unrelated because it discusses bicycles in a passage about food.#Incorrect. The passage is about bicycle safety, so the evidence is related.
~D. The evidence proves bike lanes solve every city problem.#Incorrect. The evidence supports cyclist safety, not every city issue.
}
